            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus understands 'the life' (especially in Johannine literature) as eternal, divine life imparted to believers, or as a Christological title referring to Jesus Himself as the uncreated source of this life.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines 'the life' as covenant existence—specifically, the life that is produced by obedience to the law.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Eternal divine life / Christ Himself.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Covenant existence produced by the law.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book applies the definition of covenant existence and law-keeping to New Testament passages (like John 14:6) that the consensus universally reserves for eternal divine life or Christ's person, replacing the standard referent entirely.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus identifies 'the life' as divine, eternal life found in Christ, often in contrast to what the law can provide. The book explicitly identifies it as the covenant existence that the law produces, contradicting the widely held view it mentions in its notes.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book explicitly rejects the common view (Christ Himself and eternal life) in favor of identifying 'the life' as the existence produced by the law. Applying this to passages like John 14:6 fundamentally shifts the referent from Christ's person/divine life to law-produced covenant existence.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"New Testament theology frequently contrasts the life given by Christ and the Spirit with the law, explicitly stating that the law cannot impart life (e.g., Galatians 3:21). Applying 'the life the law produces' to John 14:6 strains the immediate and broader canonical context.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
